\ The transient-frame exemption holds at a compiled call site, not just an interpreted one. Each
\ word body below compiles under eager and in an AOT binary, and each shadows a prelude word from
\ inside a called quotation.
\
\ None of the shadowing definitions is ever called. A body that calls its own nested definition is
\ not AOT-compilable, so a call would turn these runtime-legality pins into build failures.

\ `call` over a literal quotation.
shadow-via-call: ( -- ) [ [ nip: ( -- y ) [ 1 ] ; ] call ] ;

\ An `if` branch, which the compiler splices the same way.
shadow-via-if: ( -- ) [ t [ over: ( -- y ) [ 2 ] ; ] [ ] if ] ;

\ The definition sits in a callee's body. The callee brackets itself in a frame of its own, so the
\ exemption holds however `(definer)` is reached, and the quotation's frame around it is the
\ compiled call site's own bracket.
(definer): ( -- ) [ 2dup: ( -- y ) [ 3 ] ; ] ;
shadow-via-callee: ( -- ) [ [ (definer) ] call ] ;

shadow-via-call
shadow-via-if
shadow-via-callee

"after" print-line
